  "preview": "A dime is 1.35 mm thick. How tall, in centimeters, is a stack of $25 in dimes?",
  "body": {
    "goal": "Multiply rational numbers, convert between metric units (mm to cm to m), and reason about how a stack height grows linearly with the number of coins.",
    "studentPrompt": "A U.S. dime is 1.35 mm thick. There are 10 mm in 1 cm and 100 cm in 1 m.\n\n1. How tall, in centimeters, is a stack of $25 in dimes?\n2. Your friend says, 'My stack is exactly 12.15 cm tall.' How much money, in dollars, is in her stack? Show your reasoning.\n3. To reach the height of a standard doorknob (about 1 meter off the floor), how much money would you need to stack?\n4. Sketch a graph: stack height (cm) on the y-axis, dollar amount on the x-axis. What does the slope of your graph mean?",
    "image": {
      "kind": "url",
      "url": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/8844580/pexels-photo-8844580.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
      "alt": "Pile of American coins with a focus on a quarter, symbolizing currency and wealth.",
      "credit": "Photo by VOLKAN  SORKUN on Pexels"
    },
    "launch": "Pass around a single dime if you have one. Ask: 'Could you stack enough dimes to reach the ceiling? How much money would that be?' Let kids guess. Then introduce the actual thickness and the $25 prompt.",
    "materials": [
      "Vertical whiteboards",
      "Markers",
      "Optional: real dimes",
      "Optional: ruler or meter stick"
    ],
    "concepts": [
      "unit-conversion",
      "rational-multiplication",
      "proportional-reasoning",
      "linear-relationships",
      "real-world-application"
    ],
    "taskType": "investigation",
    "btcFit": "Good BTC task with a clean low floor (Q1 is one multiplication and a conversion) and rising ceiling (Q3 brings in m, Q4 brings in slope and units of cm per dollar). The reverse problem in Q2 forces students to undo the multiplication.",
    "anticipatedApproaches": "- Q1: $25 = 250 dimes. 250 × 1.35 mm = 337.5 mm = 33.75 cm.\n- Q2: 12.15 cm = 121.5 mm. 121.5 / 1.35 = 90 dimes = $9.00. Some groups set up a proportion (33.75 cm / $25 = 12.15 cm / x); both work.\n- Q3: 1 m = 1000 mm. 1000 / 1.35 ≈ 740.74 dimes. Need 741 dimes to REACH, so $74.10. (Some groups will round to $74.07 by saying 740 dimes = 999 mm — close, but under.)\n- Q4: Slope is 1.35 mm per dime, or equivalently 0.135 cm per dime, or 1.35 cm per dollar (since 10 dimes = $1). The graph is a line through the origin.",
    "commonMisconceptions": "- Treating $25 as 25 dimes instead of 250 dimes.\n- Mixing units: leaving the answer in mm and labeling it 'cm.'\n- For Q3, rounding 740.74 DOWN to 740 (740 dimes are 999 mm, just SHORT of 1 m). Need to round up.\n- For Q4, saying the slope is '1.35' without units — 1.35 of what per what?",
    "discussionQuestions": "- Two groups got 33.75 — one in mm, one in cm. Same digits. Why are both wrong if labeled the same?\n- For Q3, did you round up or down? Defend the choice.\n- The slope is 1.35 cm per dollar. What's a different unit pair you could express the slope in? Is it 'the same' rate?",
    "extensions": "- A roll of dimes holds $5 (50 dimes). The wrapper is 0.1 mm thick on each end. How tall is a full roll, in cm? How tall is a stack of 10 rolls?\n- Compare: a nickel is 1.95 mm thick. Which is taller — $25 in dimes or $25 in nickels? By how much? (Careful — there are fewer nickels per dollar.)\n- The tallest building in the world is the Burj Khalifa at about 828 m. How much money in dimes would you need to stack to match it? Express your answer in scientific notation.",
    "sampleSolutions": "1. $25 = 250 dimes. Height = 250 × 1.35 = 337.5 mm = 33.75 cm.\n2. 12.15 cm = 121.5 mm. Dimes = 121.5 / 1.35 = 90. Money = 90 × $0.10 = $9.00.\n3. 1 m = 100 cm = 1000 mm. Dimes = 1000 / 1.35 ≈ 740.74, so round up to 741 dimes. Money = $74.10. (740 dimes only reach 999 mm — JUST short.)\n4. Slope = 1.35 cm per dollar. Line through origin: y (cm) = 1.35 × x (dollars). Slope means 'each additional dollar of dimes adds 1.35 cm of height.'",
